<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Operation Faustschlag</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Operation Faustschlag was the German and Austro-Hungarian offensive on the Eastern Front in February and March 1918, launched after Bolshevik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> broke off peace talks. In eleven days German troops advanced against little resistance, occupying Latvia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. The offensive forced the Bolsheviks to accept the harsher terms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> on 3 March 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/operation-faustschlag.jpg" alt="Operation Faustschlag" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: K.u.K. Kriegspressequartier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamianets-Podilskyi-1918.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
